00:00:00,000 --> 00:00:07,280
Perfection is impossible. In the 1,526 singles matches, I played in my career, I won.

2
00:00:08,320 --> 00:00:15,760
Almost 80% of those matches. Now, I have a question for you. What percentage of points do you think

3
00:00:15,760 --> 00:00:24,080
I want in those matches? Only 54% in other words, even top ranked tennis players,

4
00:00:24,080 --> 00:00:30,240
when barely more than half of the points they play. When you lose every second point on average,

5
00:00:30,800 --> 00:00:36,640
you learn not to dwell on every shot. You teach yourself to think, okay, I double fault it,

6
00:00:36,640 --> 00:00:41,840
it's only a point. Okay, I came to the net and I got passed again. It's only a point.

7
00:00:42,480 --> 00:00:48,320
Even a great shot, an overhead back-and-smash that ends up on ESPN's top 10 play list,

8
00:00:48,320 --> 00:00:53,520
that too, is just a point. So here's why I'm telling you this. When you're playing a point,

9
00:00:54,080 --> 00:00:58,560
it has to be the most important thing in the world than it is. But when it's behind you,

10
00:00:58,560 --> 00:01:05,440
it's behind you. This mindset is really crucial because it frees you to fully commit to the next point

11
00:01:05,440 --> 00:01:12,480
at the next point after that. With intensity, clarity and focus.

